Title: Why is the (virtual) destructor of the derived class not called when deleting an array through a pointer to the base class?.
Body: <p>I have an <code>Animal</code> class with a virtual destructor, and a derived class <code>Cat</code>.</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;

struct Animal
{
    Animal() { std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Animal constructor&quot; &lt;&lt; std::endl; }
    virtual ~Animal() { std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Animal destructor&quot; &lt;&lt; std::endl; }
};

struct Cat : public Animal
{
    Cat() { std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Cat constructor&quot; &lt;&lt; std::endl; }
    ~Cat() override { std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Cat destructor&quot; &lt;&lt; std::endl; }
};

int main()
{
    const Animal *j = new Cat[1];
    delete[] j;
}
</code></pre>
<p>This gives the output:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Animal constructor  <br />
Cat constructor  <br />
Animal destructor</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I don't understand why is the <code>Cat</code>'s destructor not called, when my base class destructor is virtual?</p>

Title: Are multidimensional array accesses sequenced?.
Body: <p>Take the following:</p>
<pre><code>int a(void) {
    puts(&quot;a&quot;);
    return 0;
}

int b(void) {
    puts(&quot;b&quot;);
    return 1;
}

int c(void) {
    puts(&quot;c&quot;);
    return 2;
}

int d(void) {
    puts(&quot;d&quot;);
    return 3;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Will the following have predictable behavior?</p>
<pre><code>int arr[4][4][4][4];
arr[a()][b()][c()][d()] = 1;
</code></pre>
<p>Is it guaranteed to print in this order:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>a
b
c
d
</code></pre>
<p>I am aware that constructs such as the following are invalid:</p>
<pre><code>int i;
i = i++;
</code></pre>
<p>This is because <code>=</code> is an unsequenced operator, so whether <code>i</code> or <code>i++</code> is evaluated first is undefined. It is undefined behavior to access and modify a single object before another sequence point.</p>
<p>Put another way, is the following valid:</p>
<pre><code>int i = 0, arr[4][4][4][4];
arr[i++][i++][i++][i++] = 1;
</code></pre>
<p>Or does it invoke undefined behavior due to unsequenced modification and access to <code>i</code>?</p>
<p>According to the C standard, is there a defined sequence point between each successive <code>[]</code> while indexing a multidimensional array?</p>
<p>To be clear, neither of these examples have to do with precedence, the placement of implicit parenthesis, the order in which the operators operate on their operands. The question is about sequencing, the order in which the <em>operands themselves</em> are evaluated.</p>

Title: std::sort invocation causes subscript out of range compile-time error.
Body: <p>The below code fails to compile with error: &quot;array subscript 16 is outside array bounds of …&quot;.
I don't understand why, <code>w</code> is guaranteed to be &lt;= arr1.size().</p>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/5n5KohsnW" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://godbolt.org/z/5n5KohsnW</a></p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;array&gt;
#include &lt;algorithm&gt;
#include &lt;tuple&gt;

extern bool test_func();

int tempfunc() 
{
    std::array&lt;std::tuple&lt;double,int&gt;,5&gt; arr1;
    int w = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 5; ++i ) {
        if( test_func()) {
            arr1[w] = {3.,4};
            w++;
        }
    }
    std::sort(arr1.begin(), arr1.begin() + w);

    return 0;
}
</code></pre>

answer:
The provided C++ code snippet contains a runtime error due to an attempt to access an element in an `std::array` using an index that exceeds its size. This results in a "subscript out of range" compile-time error.

Here's a breakdown of the issue:

1. **Initialization and Assignment**:
   ```cpp
   std::array<std::tuple<double,int>,5> arr1;
   int w = 0;
   for (int i = 0; i < 5; ++i ) {
       if( test_func()) {
           arr1[w] = {3.,4}; // This line can cause an out-of-range error
           w++;
       }
   }
   ```
   - The variable `w` is initialized to 0.
   - A loop runs from 0 to 4.
   - Inside the loop, it checks the result of `test_func()`.
   - If `test_func()` returns true, it assigns a tuple `{3.,4}` to `arr1[w]`, where `w` is incremented by 1.

2. **Sorting**:
   ```cpp
   std::sort(arr1.begin(), arr1.begin() + w);
   ```
   - The `std::sort` function sorts the elements of the first `w` elements of `arr1`.

3. **Runtime Error**:
   - When the `for` loop finishes, `w` will have a value greater than or equal to 5 because `test_func()` returned true at least once for each iteration.
   - However, `std::sort` expects the input range `[begin(), begin() + w)`, which means it should sort up to but not including `arr1[w]`.
   - Since `w` can be as large as 5, attempting to sort up to `arr1[5]` leads to an out-of-range error.

To fix this issue, ensure that the `w` index does not exceed the size of the array before sorting. Here's the corrected code:

```cpp
#include <array>
#include <algorithm>
#include <tuple>

extern bool test_func();

int tempfunc() 
{
    std::array<std::tuple<double,int>,5> arr1;
    int w = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < 5; ++i ) {
        if( test_func()) {
            arr1[w] = {3.,4};
            w++;
        }
    }
    // Ensure w does not exceed arr1.size()
    if (w > arr1.size()) {
        throw std::out_of_range("Array index out of bounds");
    }
    std::sort(arr1.begin(), arr1.begin() + w);

    return 0;
}
```

In this modified version, we added a check after sorting to ensure that `w` does not exceed the size of `arr1`. If `w` is larger, we throw an exception, which prevents the program from accessing an out-of-range index and causing a runtime error.

