Title: Is gcc wrong to allow the initialization of a const array member with another array reference?.
Body: <p>While (re)implementing a simple constexpr map, I wrote this
(<a href="https://gcc.godbolt.org/z/6TnKn6zx4" rel="nofollow noreferrer">godbolt</a>):</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>template &lt;class key_type, class value_type, int N&gt;
class flat_map
{
private:
    struct pair
    {
        key_type key;
        value_type value;
    };
    const pair elements[N];

public:
    consteval flat_map(const pair (&amp;arr)[N]) noexcept
        : elements(arr) // works on gcc?!
    {}

    [[nodiscard]] consteval value_type operator[](const key_type key) const
    {
        for (const pair &amp;elem : elements)
            if (elem.key == key)
                return elem.value;
        throw &quot;Key not found&quot;;
    }
};

constexpr flat_map&lt;int, char, 3&gt; m = {{
    { 4, 'a' }, { -1, 'b' }, { 42, 'c' }
}};
static_assert(m[4] == 'a');
static_assert(m[-1] == 'b');
static_assert(m[42] == 'c');

int main()
{
    return m[4]; // 97=='a'
}
</code></pre>
<p>I naively thought to set the private array <code>elements</code> as <code>const</code> and initialize it in the constructor; I was using <em>gcc</em> trunk as compiler, and all was seemingly working well.
When I decided to try it with <em>msvc</em> and <em>clang</em>, I had compilation errors: both were complaining about the array initialization requiring a brace-enclosed initializer list.</p>
<p>In hindsight the other compilers aren't particularly wrong, are they?
Am I inadvertently using some <em>gcc</em> non standard extensions here?</p>
<p>Ehm, by the way, what would you do to avoid copying the array elements by hand?</p>

Title: why std::pair&lt;int[N], int[N]&gt; is not allowed in C++?.
Body: <p>Why the C++ code below can't be compiled?</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;utility&gt;

int main() {
    int x[6];
    int y[6];
    std::pair&lt;int[6], int[6]&gt; a(x, y);
    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>For example MSVC gives the following error:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>error C2661: 'std::pair&lt;int [6],int [6]&gt;::pair': no overloaded function takes 2 argument</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Thanks for the comments that using C++ alternatives to construct arrays, but I also want to know the reason that the code can't be compiled.</p>

answer:
The code cannot be compiled because `std::pair` does not accept an array as its first template parameter. Instead, it expects a pair of objects or a pair of pointers. An array cannot be directly used as an object.

To create a `std::pair` with two arrays, you need to use a container like `std::vector`. Here's how you can modify your code:

```cpp
#include <utility>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    int x[6];
    int y[6];
    std::vector<int> vec_x(x, x + 6);
    std::vector<int> vec_y(y, y + 6);
    std::pair<std::vector<int>, std::vector<int>> a(vec_x, vec_y);
    return 0;
}
```

In this modified code:
- We define two `std::vector<int>` instances `vec_x` and `vec_y`, which contain the elements of `x` and `y`.
- We then create a `std::pair` `a` where the first element is `vec_x` and the second element is `vec_y`.

This approach allows you to use arrays as elements within a `std::pair` by wrapping them in appropriate containers.

