Title: Why some character can&#39;t be edited in C++?.
Body: <p>I'm trying to write my own <code>strcat</code> function in C++, but it has some problems.<br />
My input is two chars <code>c</code> and <code>a</code>, and my function will return a char pointer to a char which <code>c</code> is concatenated with <code>a</code>.</p>
<p>For example,<br />
Input: <code>'abc'</code> <code>'xyz'</code><br />
Expected Output: <code>'xyzabc'</code><br />
My function's Ouput: <code>'xyza@▲∩'</code></p>
<p>My function returns some special character different to my input.</p>
<p>I debugged my function and found that:</p>
<ul>
<li>When <code>i=0</code>, <code>destination[3]</code> = <code>source[0]</code> = <code>'a'</code></li>
<li>But when <code>i=1</code>, <code>destination[8]</code> = <code>source[1]</code> = <code>'b'</code></li>
<li>And when <code>i=2</code>, <code>destination[9]</code> = <code>source[2]</code> = <code>'c'</code></li>
<li>Finally, <code>destination[10]</code> = <code>'\0'</code></li>
</ul>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>#include&lt;iostream&gt;
#include&lt;string.h&gt;
using namespace std;

char* mystrcat ( char * destination, const char *source){
    for (int i=0; i&lt;strlen(source); i++) {
        destination[strlen(destination)+i] = source[i];
    }
    destination[strlen(destination)+strlen(source)]='\0';
    return destination;
}

int main() {
    char c[100];
    cin.getline(c, 99);
    char a[100];
    cin.getline(a,99);

    mystrcat(a,c);
    cout&lt;&lt;a;
    return 0;
}
</code></pre>

answer:
The issue lies in how you're handling the destination string. You're appending each character of the `source` string to the end of the `destination` string using the `+=` operator. However, this approach doesn't properly handle the null terminator (`'\0'`) at the end of the `destination` string.

Here's the corrected version of your code:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <cstring>

char* mystrcat(char *destination, const char *source) {
    int destLen = strlen(destination);
    int srcLen = strlen(source);

    // Ensure there's enough space for the null terminator
    if (destLen + srcLen >= sizeof(destination)) {
        throw "Insufficient space in destination buffer";
    }

    // Copy characters from source to destination
    for (int i = 0; i < srcLen; i++) {
        destination[destLen + i] = source[i];
    }

    // Null terminate the destination string
    destination[destLen + srcLen] = '\0';

    return destination;
}

int main() {
    char c[100];
    cin.getline(c, 99);
    char a[100];
    cin.getline(a, 99);

    try {
        mystrcat(a, c);
        cout << a;
    } catch (const char* e) {
        cerr << "Error: " << e << endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In this corrected version:
1. We first determine the length of the existing `destination` string and the `source` string.
2. We check if there's enough space in the `destination` buffer to accommodate the new characters from the `source` string plus the null terminator.
3. If there isn't enough space, we throw an exception.
4. If there is enough space, we copy the characters from the `source` string to the end of the `destination` string.
5. Finally, we null terminate the `destination` string to ensure it's properly terminated.

This should resolve the issue where the output contains unexpected characters.

