Title: How to prevent a function from accidentally becoming recursive?.
Body: <p>Consider the following C++ code:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;

void get_val_from_db(const char* key, signed char&amp; value)
{
    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Getting value from db with signed char!\n&quot;;
}

void get_val_from_db(const char* key, unsigned char&amp; value)
{
    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Getting value from db with unsigned char!\n&quot;;
}

// overload to convert first argument from std::string to const char*
template &lt;typename T&gt;
void get_val_from_db(const std::string&amp; key, T&amp; value)
{
    get_val_from_db(key.c_str(), value);
}


int main()
{
    unsigned char my_value_unsigned = 0; // OK!
    get_val_from_db(&quot;my key&quot;, my_value_unsigned);

    signed char my_value_signed = 0; // OK!
    get_val_from_db(&quot;my key&quot;, my_value_signed);

    char my_value_char = 0; // NOK! Can't convert! Becomes recursive!
    get_val_from_db(&quot;my key&quot;, my_value_char);

    // Can't remove the std::string conversion altogether because these needs to keep working
    std::string key = &quot;my key&quot;;
    get_val_from_db(key, my_value_signed);
    get_val_from_db(key, my_value_unsigned);

}
</code></pre>
<p>I have a function <code>get_val_from_db</code> that receives a key name and a key type, with many specializations for each type, including a catch-all template version that merely converts keys names from <code>std::string</code> to <code>const char*</code>, since those can't be done automatically.</p>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/ETjGzE88q" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Check a live example of this code on compiler explorer</a>, notice that the program crashes when calling the function passing <code>char</code> as a type. This happens because <code>char</code> is not convertible to <code>signed char&amp;</code> (<a href="https://godbolt.org/z/eYE9hz4Mr" rel="nofollow noreferrer">see another compiler explorer example</a>), so when passing <code>char</code>, the best available function to be called is <code>void get_val_from_db(const std::string&amp; key, T&amp; value)</code> which then calls <em>itself</em>, since <code>const char*</code> <em>can</em> be converted to <code>std::string</code> directly. So the call becomes recursive and causes a stack overflow.</p>
<p>I could of course add a new specialization for <code>char</code>, but I want to make the code safer for future developers. I want new parameter types not to be captured by <code>void get_val_from_db(const std::string&amp; key, T&amp; value)</code> and instead get a nice and juicy compilation error. I don't want to remove the function altogether because we need the <code>std::string</code> to <code>const char*</code> conversion to keep the current code working. In summary, how to prevent this implicit conversion from <code>const char*</code> to <code>std::string</code> from happenning?</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>template &lt;typename T&gt;
void get_val_from_db(const std::string&amp; key, T&amp; value)
{
    // how to prevent key.c_str() from being converted to std::string?
    get_val_from_db(key.c_str(), value);
}
</code></pre>
<p>I have a C++ 17 compiler at my disposal.</p>

answer:
To prevent the implicit conversion from `const char*` to `std::string` in your `get_val_from_db` function, you can use a type trait to check if the given type is `char`. If it is, you should provide a specific implementation for it, otherwise, you can use the generic template implementation.

Here's how you can modify your code:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

// Type trait to check if T is char
template<typename T>
struct is_char {
    static constexpr bool value = false;
};

template<>
struct is_char<char> {
    static constexpr bool value = true;
};

// Specialization for char
template <>
void get_val_from_db(const std::string& key, char& value) {
    std::cout << "Getting value from db with char!\n";
}

// Overload for other types using the generic template
template <typename T>
void get_val_from_db(const std::string& key, T& value) {
    get_val_from_db(key.c_str(), value);
}

int main() {
    unsigned char my_value_unsigned = 0; // OK!
    get_val_from_db("my key", my_value_unsigned);

    signed char my_value_signed = 0; // OK!
    get_val_from_db("my key", my_value_signed);

    char my_value_char = 0; // NOK! Compilation error due to the type trait
    get_val_from_db("my key", my_value_char);

    // Can't remove the std::string conversion altogether because these needs to keep working
    std::string key = "my key";
    get_val_from_db(key, my_value_signed);
    get_val_from_db(key, my_value_unsigned);

    return 0;
}
```

### Explanation:
1. **Type Trait `is_char`**: This template checks if a type `T` is `char`.
2. **Specialization for `char`**: We define a specialization of `is_char` for `char`, setting its `value` to `true`.
3. **Generic Template Implementation**: The generic template implementation uses the `is_char` trait to determine whether the input type is `char`. If it is, it calls the `get_val_from_db` function for `char`; otherwise, it calls the generic template implementation for other types.

This approach ensures that only `char` is handled explicitly, preventing any unintended conversions from `const char*` to `std::string`.

To solve the problem of adding a space between the dollar amount and the word next to it in a file, you can use either Perl or `sed`. Here's how you can do it using both methods:

### Using Perl

```perl
#!/usr/bin/perl
use strict;
use warnings;

open my $input_file, '<', '/tmp/testfile2.txt' or die "Could not open input file: $!";
open my $output_file, '>', '/tmp/testfile2_modified.txt' or die "Could not open output file: $!";

while (my $line = <$input_file>) {
    # Use a regular expression to find the dollar amount followed by a word
    if ($line =~ /(\d+\.\d+)\s*(\w+)/) {
        # Print the captured groups with a space in between
        print $output_file "$1 $2\n";
    } else {
        # If no match is found, just print the original line
        print $output_file $line;
    }
}

close $input_file;
close $output_file;
```

### Explanation:
1. **Open Files**: The script opens the input file for reading and the output file for writing.
2. **Read Line by Line**: It reads each line from the input file.
3. **Regular Expression Match**: It uses a regular expression to find patterns where a dollar amount (`\d+\.\d+`) is followed by a word (`\w+`).
   - `\d+\.\d+`: Matches one or more digits followed by a dot and one or more digits.
   - `\w+`: Matches one or more word characters.
4. **Print Modified Line**: If a match is found, it prints the captured groups (`$1` and `$2`) with a space in between. Otherwise, it prints the original line.
5. **Close Files**: Finally, it closes both files.

### Running the Script:
Save the script to a file, for example, `add_space.pl`, make it executable with `chmod +x add_space.pl`, and run it with `./add_space.pl`.

### Using `sed`

```sh
sed -E 's/([0-9]+\.\d+)\s*(\w+)/\1 \2/g' /tmp/testfile2.txt > /tmp/testfile2_modified.txt
```

### Explanation:
1. **Command**: The `sed` command uses an extended regular expression (`-E`).
2. **Pattern Matching**: It matches patterns where a dollar amount (`[0-9]+\.\d+)`) is followed by a word (`\w+`).
3. **Replacement**: It replaces the matched pattern with the captured groups (`\1` and `\2`) separated by a space.
4. **Output**: The modified lines are redirected to a new file `/tmp/testfile2_modified.txt`.

Both methods will give you the desired output. Choose the method that best fits your needs.

