The issue you're encountering is due to the fact that Zod expects a number for the `age` field, but the user is submitting a string. You can fix this by ensuring that the `handleChange` function for the `age` input correctly parses the input as a number.

Here's how you can modify your `App` component to handle this:

```javascript
import React, { useState } from "react";
import { CardData, defaultCardData, handleSubmit } from "./validation/zod";
import Input from "./components/Input";

function App() {
  // state date for form
  const [data, setData] = useState<CardData>(defaultCardData);
  // loading and function
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);
  const isLoading = (val: boolean) => setLoading(val);
  // error and function
  const [error, setError] = useState(false);
  const isError = (val: boolean) => setError(val);

  const handleChange = (e: { target: { value: string | number } }) => {
    if (typeof e.target.value === "string") {
      setData({ ...data, age: parseInt(e.target.value, 10) });
    } else {
      setData({ ...data, age: e.target.value });
    }
  };

  return (
    <main className="min-h-screen flex justify-center flex-col items-center text-white">
      <h1 className="text-2xl font-bold">Card Data</h1>

      <form
        className="text-black"
        onSubmit={(event) => handleSubmit(event, data, isError, isLoading)}
      >
        {/* //first name */}
        <Input
          label="First Name "
          id="firstName"
          value={data.firstName}
          handleChange={(e: { target: { value: string } }) => {
            setData({ ...data, firstName: e.target.value });
          }}
        />
        {/* //age */}
        <Input
          label="Age "
          id="age"
          value={data.age}
          type="number"
          handleChange={handleChange}
        />
        {/* //submit */}
        <button
          className="block mt-4 p-2 bg-white border-black border-2 rounded-md"
          type="submit"
        >
          Submit
        </button>
        {loading && <p>Loading...</p>}
        {error && <p>Error on submission</p>}
      </form>
    </main>
  );
}

export default App;
```

In this modified code, the `handleChange` function now checks whether the input value is a string or a number. If it's a string, it attempts to parse it using `parseInt`, setting the `age` state with the parsed number. This ensures that Zod can correctly validate the input as a number.

To resolve the issue with `[attr.disabled]=true` not working in an Angular 15 reactive form, you need to ensure that Angular correctly handles boolean attributes like `disabled`. In Angular 15, this requires the use of the `@Input()` decorator for boolean properties.

Here's how you can modify your component to make sure the `disabled` attribute works correctly:

```typescript
import { Component } from '@angular/core';
import { FormBuilder, FormGroup } from '@angular/forms';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-my-form',
  templateUrl: './my-form.component.html',
  styleUrls: ['./my-form.component.css']
})
export class MyFormComponent {
  myForm: FormGroup;

  constructor(private fb: FormBuilder) {
    this.myForm = this.fb.group({
      disableField: false // Initialize with a default value
    });
  }

  toggleDisable() {
    this.myForm.get('disableField').setValue(!this.myForm.get('disableField').value);
  }
}
```

In the template file (`my-form.component.html`), you can bind the `disabled` attribute directly to the form control:

```html
<form [formGroup]="myForm">
  <label>
    Disable Field:
    <input type="checkbox" [(ngModel)]="myForm.get('disableField').value">
  </label>
  <button (click)="toggleDisable()">Toggle Disabled</button>
  <input type="text" formControlName="yourFormControl"> <!-- Replace with your actual form control name -->
</form>
```

By using the `@Input()` decorator on the `disableField` property of the form group, Angular will properly handle the boolean attribute and apply the correct styling. This approach ensures compatibility between Angular versions 14 and 15.

