Title: Proper way to create a controlled `&lt;input type=&#39;file&#39;&gt;` element in React.
Body: <p>Most <code>&lt;input&gt;</code> elements use a <code>value</code> prop to set the value, and so they can be externally controlled by a parent.</p>
<p>However <code>&lt;input type='file'&gt;</code> sets its value from a <code>files</code> attribute, and I'm struggling to make that work correctly. <code>files</code> can not be set directly as a prop, but it can be set on the DOM directly via a ref, so I use <code>useEffect()</code> to accomplish this:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>import React, { useEffect, useRef } from &quot;react&quot;;

const FileInput = (props) =&gt; {
  const { value } = props;
  const inputRef = useRef();

  useEffect(() =&gt; {
    if (value === &quot;&quot;) {
      inputRef.current.value = &quot;&quot;;
    } else {
      inputRef.current.files = value;
    }
  }, [value]);

  return &lt;input type=&quot;file&quot; ref={inputRef} /&gt;;
};

export default FileInput;
</code></pre>
<p>I'd like to include an <code>onChange()</code> handler for when the user selects a file, but the <code>&lt;FileList&gt;</code> object is tied to the DOM and I get an error when trying to use it to set the <code>value</code>:</p>
<pre><code>DOMException: An attempt was made to use an object that is not, or is no longer, usable
</code></pre>
<p>I keep going in circles on the &quot;right&quot; way to write a controlled form input. Is there a way to set the <code>files</code> attribute and <code>value</code> attribute correctly?</p>
<p>Thanks!</p>

answer:
To properly control a `<input type="file">` element in React, you should use a combination of state management and the `onChange` event. Here's how you can do it:

1. Use the `useState` hook to manage the file selection state.
2. Create a reference to the input element using the `useRef` hook.
3. Use the `useEffect` hook to update the file selection based on the state.
4. Implement an `onChange` event handler to update the state when the user selects a file.

Here's the complete code:

```javascript
import React, { useState, useRef } from "react";

const FileInput = ({ onChange }) => {
  const [selectedFiles, setSelectedFiles] = useState(null);
  const inputRef = useRef();

  // Update selected files based on the state
  useEffect(() => {
    if (selectedFiles !== null) {
      inputRef.current.files = selectedFiles;
    }
  }, [selectedFiles]);

  // Handle file selection change
  const handleFileChange = (event) => {
    setSelectedFiles(event.target.files);
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <label htmlFor="file-input">Select a file:</label>
      <input
        type="file"
        id="file-input"
        ref={inputRef}
        onChange={handleFileChange}
      />
      {selectedFiles && (
        <ul>
          {Array.from(selectedFiles).map((file, index) => (
            <li key={index}>{file.name}</li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      )}
    </div>
  );
};

export default FileInput;
```

### Explanation:
1. **State Management**: The `useState` hook is used to manage the `selectedFiles` state. This state will hold the list of files selected by the user.
2. **Reference to Input Element**: The `useRef` hook creates a mutable reference to the input element, which allows us to interact with the DOM directly.
3. **Effect Hook**: The `useEffect` hook is used to update the file selection whenever the `selectedFiles` state changes. It checks if `selectedFiles` has been updated and updates the `files` property of the input element accordingly.
4. **Event Handler**: The `handleFileChange` function is called whenever the user selects a file. It updates the `selectedFiles` state with the new file list.
5. **Rendering**: The component renders a label and an input element. The `ref` attribute is used to bind the input element to the reference, allowing us to access its properties. The `onChange` event is also bound to the `handleFileChange` function to update the state when the user selects a file.

This approach ensures that the `<input type="file">` element is controlled and can be dynamically updated based on user interactions.

