Title: iOS Safari autofill not populating all address fields at once.
Body: <p>I am currently working on an HTML form that includes fields for user address information such as name, street, zip, and city. The form utilizes W3C autocomplete attributes to facilitate autofill. While testing, I observed that the autofill feature works seamlessly on Android and Windows platforms, but encounters issues on iOS, specifically with Safari.</p>
<p>Here’s a simplified version of my form fields with the autocomplete attributes:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;form action=&quot;/submit&quot; method=&quot;post&quot;&gt;
  &lt;input type=&quot;text&quot; name=&quot;name&quot; autocomplete=&quot;name&quot; placeholder=&quot;Name&quot;&gt;
  &lt;input type=&quot;text&quot; name=&quot;street&quot; autocomplete=&quot;address-line1&quot; placeholder=&quot;Street&quot;&gt;
  &lt;input type=&quot;text&quot; name=&quot;city&quot; autocomplete=&quot;address-level2&quot; placeholder=&quot;City&quot;&gt;
  &lt;input type=&quot;text&quot; name=&quot;zip&quot; autocomplete=&quot;postal-code&quot; placeholder=&quot;Zip Code&quot;&gt;
  &lt;input type=&quot;submit&quot; value=&quot;Submit&quot;&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>On Safari, when a user selects a contact for autofill, the street and zip fields are not populated simultaneously with the other fields. However, when clicking into the street or zip field individually, Safari proposes the autofill contact again and fills in the data correctly.</p>
<p>I have ensured that all details of the contact information used for autofill are set to &quot;Business&quot; and contains complete and correctly formatted address information. I have also tried using both address-line1 and street-address for the street field, but the issue persists. I have tried on several iphones, everywhere the same effect.</p>
<p>I expect all available fields to be filled when the user clicks into one field, eg &quot;Name&quot; and then selects the configured autofill-contact.</p>
<p>Is this some kind of weird safety policy to not fill in everything?</p>

answer:
To resolve the issue where iOS Safari does not populate all address fields simultaneously when using W3C autocomplete attributes, you can try the following steps:

1. **Ensure Autocomplete Attributes Are Correctly Set**:
   Make sure that the autocomplete attributes for your input fields are correctly set to match the expected formats. For example, use `address-line1` for street and `postal-code` for zip.

   ```html
   &lt;input type=&quot;text&quot; name=&quot;street&quot; autocomplete=&quot;address-line1&quot; placeholder=&quot;Street&quot;&gt;
   &lt;input type=&quot;text&quot; name=&quot;zip&quot; autocomplete=&quot;postal-code&quot; placeholder=&quot;Zip Code&quot;&gt;
   ```

2. **Use `autofill` Attribute**:
   You can also use the `autofill` attribute to explicitly specify which fields should be autofilled. This can sometimes help in resolving issues where the browser might not automatically fill certain fields.

   ```html
   &lt;input type=&quot;text&quot; name=&quot;street&quot; autocomplete=&quot;address-line1&quot; placeholder=&quot;Street&quot; autocomplete=&quot;off&quot;&gt;
   &lt;input type=&quot;text&quot; name=&quot;zip&quot; autocomplete=&quot;postal-code&quot; placeholder=&quot;Zip Code&quot; autocomplete=&quot;off&quot;&gt;
   ```

3. **Check Browser Settings**:
   Sometimes, browser settings can interfere with the autofill functionality. Ensure that the browser is not blocking or restricting the autofill behavior.

4. **Test on Different Devices and Browsers**:
   Test your form on multiple devices and browsers to ensure the issue is consistent across different environments.

5. **Update Your Form and Libraries**:
   Ensure that you are using the latest versions of your form and any libraries you are using, as bugs and improvements may have been made since the last update.

6. **Customize Autofill Behavior**:
   If the above steps do not work, you might need to customize the autofill behavior by handling the `autocomplete` event and manually filling the missing fields.

Here is an example of how you could handle the `autocomplete` event to manually fill the missing fields:

```javascript
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function() {
  document.querySelector('form').addEventListener('autocomplete', function(event) {
    var formData = new FormData(this);
    if (formData.has('street')) {
      var addressLine1 = formData.get('address-line1');
      if (!addressLine1) {
        // Manually fill the street field
        event.target.elements['street'].value = "Your Street Address";
      }
    }
    if (formData.has('zip')) {
      var postalCode = formData.get('postal-code');
      if (!postalCode) {
        // Manually fill the zip field
        event.target.elements['zip'].value = "Your Zip Code";
      }
    }
  });
});
```

By following these steps, you should be able to resolve the issue with iOS Safari not populating all address fields simultaneously when using W3C autocomplete attributes.

