Title: Lazy Evaluation of the :invalid selector.
Body: <p>Normally the :invalid selector is evaluated instantly : The element will be styled immediately with :invalid for example if a value is required as soon as the page loads:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>input:invalid {
  border: 10px solid red;
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;form&gt;
&lt;p&gt; Go red only after I focused and blurred away &lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;input required placeholder="you must type something" /&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>Would there be a workaround for this so that the evaluation happens only after the user interacts with the form? (typically, after the first focus / blur).
I understand this is standard browser implementation behavior so we're likely looking for a workaround here.</p>
<p>Minimal JS is OK although I don't want to do JS validation.</p>

answer:
To achieve lazy evaluation of the `:invalid` selector in web development, you can use JavaScript to conditionally apply styling based on whether an input field is valid or invalid. Here's how you can modify the provided HTML and JavaScript code:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Lazy Evaluation of :invalid Selector</title>
    <style>
        .invalid-input {
            border: 10px solid red;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <p>Go red only after I focused and blurred away.</p>
    <form id="myForm">
        <label for="username">Username:</label>
        <input type="text" id="username" required placeholder="required">
        <button type="submit">Submit</button>
    </form>

    <script>
        const form = document.getElementById('myForm');
        const inputs = form.querySelectorAll('input');

        function validateInput(input) {
            return !input.validity.valid;
        }

        function updateInvalidClass() {
            inputs.forEach(input => {
                if (validateInput(input)) {
                    input.classList.add('invalid-input');
                } else {
                    input.classList.remove('invalid-input');
                }
            });
        }

        // Add event listeners to handle focus and blur events
        inputs.forEach(input => {
            input.addEventListener('focus', updateInvalidClass);
            input.addEventListener('blur', updateInvalidClass);
        });

        // Initial check for all inputs
        updateInvalidClass();
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

### Explanation:
1. **HTML Structure**: A simple form with an input field that is required and has a placeholder.
2. **CSS Styling**: A CSS class `.invalid-input` that changes the border color to red when applied.
3. **JavaScript**:
   - **Validation Function**: `validateInput` checks if an input field is invalid using `input.validity.valid`.
   - **Update Invalid Class Function**: `updateInvalidClass` iterates over all input fields, checks their validity, and adds or removes the `.invalid-input` class accordingly.
   - **Event Listeners**: Event listeners are added to each input field to call `updateInvalidClass` whenever the input receives focus or loses focus.

This approach ensures that the `:invalid` selector is only evaluated when the user interacts with the form, providing a more interactive user experience without requiring manual JavaScript validation.

