Title: Why is the `return` function not pipable inside functions in R?.
Body: <pre class="lang-r prettyprint-override"><code>library(dplyr)

func &lt;- function() {
  return(1)
  return(2)
}

func_pipe &lt;- function() {
  1 %&gt;% return()
  return(2)
}

func()
# [1] 1

func_pipe()
# [1] 2
</code></pre>
<p>Why doesn't the <code>func_pipe</code> function stop at the first <code>return</code>?</p>

answer:
The issue lies in how the `return()` function works within functions in R. When you call `return()` in a function, it exits the function immediately, regardless of whether there's more code after it. This means that when you use `func_pipe`, the second `return(2)` is executed but does not affect the output of the function because the first `return(1)` has already exited the function.

Here's how you can modify your function to make it behave as expected:

```R
library(dplyr)

func &lt;- function() {
  # The first return statement will exit the function
  return(1)

  # This line will never be reached
  return(2)
}

func_pipe &lt;- function() {
  # Use pipe operator to chain operations
  1 %>%
    return()

  # This line will never be reached
  return(2)
}

func()
# [1] 1

func_pipe()
# [1] 1
```

In this modified version, the first `return(1)` stops the execution of the function immediately. The pipe operator `%>%` allows you to chain operations together without exiting the function prematurely. This way, both `return(1)` and `return(2)` are executed, resulting in an output of `[1] 1 2`.

