Title: Static variable in a loop vs variable and a loop in a block.
Body: <p>In C we can use blocks to restrict the scope of a variable, so is</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>{
    int var = /* initialization */;

    while(...) {
        // some stuff with var
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>equivalent to</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>while(...) {
    static int var = /* initialization */;
    // some stuff with var
}
</code></pre>
<p>?</p>

answer:
Yes.

The two pieces of code you provided are essentially equivalent in terms of their functionality. Both snippets declare an integer variable `var` and initialize it before entering the loop. The difference lies in how the scope of this variable is maintained within the loop body.

In the first snippet:
```c
{
    int var = /* initialization */;

    while(...) {
        // some stuff with var
    }
}
```
The variable `var` has a block scope, meaning its lifetime ends at the end of the block where it is declared. This means that `var` is not accessible outside of the curly braces `{}`.

In the second snippet:
```c
while(...) {
    static int var = /* initialization */;
    // some stuff with var
}
```
The variable `var` also has a block scope, but it is declared with the `static` keyword. A static local variable retains its value between function calls, which means that the initial value assigned to `var` (if any) is preserved across multiple iterations of the loop. This allows `var` to maintain its state within each iteration.

Therefore, both snippets achieve the same result: they declare and initialize an integer variable `var`, and then use it inside a loop without needing to reinitialize it for each iteration. The choice between using a block scope or a static local variable typically depends on whether you need to retain the state of the variable across multiple function calls or if you prefer a more explicit control over the variable's lifetime.

