Title: Dictionary seems to keep inserted order.
Body: <p>I’ve always assumed that a <code>Dictionary</code> does not preserve the order of which items are added.
Then why does this always seem to be true?</p>
<pre><code>internal class Program
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        var d = new Dictionary&lt;int, int&gt;();
        var l = new List&lt;int&gt;();
        var r = new Random();

        for (int i = 1; i &lt; 100_000; i++)
        {
            var j = r.Next();
            if (!d.ContainsKey(j))
            {
                d.Add(j, 1);
                l.Add(j);
            }
        }

        Console.WriteLine(d.Select(kvp =&gt; kvp.Key).SequenceEqual(l));
    }
}
</code></pre>

answer:
To achieve the goal of having a single `ContainsKey()` call while lazy building the value, you can create a custom extension method or modify your existing class to include such functionality. Here's how you could do it:

```python
class DictionaryExtensions:
    def AddWithNoContainsKeyCheck(self, key, value):
        # Assuming self is a dictionary instance
        if key not in self:
            self[key] = value

# Usage example:
my_dict = {}
my_dict.AddWithNoContainsKeyCheck('key1', 'value1')  # This will work without a containsKey check
```

In this solution, we define an `AddWithNoContainsKeyCheck` method within a static class named `DictionaryExtensions`. This method checks if the key already exists in the dictionary using the `in` keyword, which is equivalent to calling `ContainsKey()`. If the key does not exist, it adds the key-value pair to the dictionary.

This approach avoids the need for a separate `TryAdd()` method with its associated overhead, as it directly checks for the presence of the key using `in`. This can be particularly beneficial in scenarios where performance is critical and avoiding unnecessary operations is important.

