Title: How to programatically force Windows to update a file&#39;s reported on-disk size, while the file is being written?.
Body: <p>I've got a program that opens an ASCII log file and writes lines of text to the end of it whenever events occur.  The timing of the events is unpredictable; they may happen many times per second, or they may only happen once every few hours.</p>
<p>This all works fine; the problem comes when I want to see what the current size of the log file is, while the program is running.  Under Windows, if I enter <code>dir</code> into a DOS shell, the log file's size shows up as 0 bytes, until such time as my program closes the log file.  (Interestingly, if I do a <code>dir my_file_name.log</code>, the file's size updates, but just a plain <code>dir</code> does not update the file size)</p>
<p>This is less useful than I would like; however according to <a href="https://superuser.com/questions/727883/why-some-write-in-progress-files-report-0-byte-but-shrinking-drives-free-space">this  question/answer on SuperUser.com</a>, that behavior is a &quot;feature&quot; of the Windows OS.  The answer even gives a hack work-around to force the file-size figure to update (by entering <code>type my_file_name.log &gt; nul</code> into the DOS box).</p>
<p>My question is, is there some programmatic way to force that same update from within my program?  I'd like to be able to at least occasionally force the correct size to be shown, without having to tell the user to manually perform a work-around step.</p>
<p>I tried simulating the behavior of <code>type</code> from within my program, by doing a separate call to <code>fopen()</code> and <code>fclose()</code> on my log file's filepath, but it didn't seem to have any effect.</p>
<p>I also tried forcing the issue by calling <code>fclose()</code> on my log file's file-handle, and then re-opening the file in append-mode immediately afterwards (via <code>fopen()</code> again), but for some reason the <code>fopen()</code> call failed.  (I don't really want to do this anyway, for precisely the reason that re-opening the file might fail, leaving my program in an undesirable state)</p>
<p>See toy example program below:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

