Title: c++ read data from file and convert to template type of class.
Body: <p>Following case:</p>
<pre><code>template &lt;typename NumberType = double&gt;
class A
{
   //constructur, destructor,...

   //member variable
   std::vector&lt;std::vector&lt;NumberType&gt; matrix_;

   //member function
   void read(std::ifstream fileIn)
   {
       std::string line, word;
       unsigned int row = 0;
       while (std::getline(fileIn, line))
       {
         std::istringstream lineStream(line);
         unsigned int col = 0;
         while (std::getline(lineStream, word, ','))
            {
               matrix_[row][col] = std::stod(word); // how to convert to NumberType?
               col++;
            }
         row++;
      }
   }
};
</code></pre>
<p>I read matrix-like data from a csv file and want to store the entries in a container, whose type is a template type from a class. At the moment, I instantiate the class only for <code>NumberType = double</code> , so I hard-coded <code>std::stod(word)</code> for testing.</p>
<p>But how can I convert the string <code>word</code> to <code>NumberType</code>? NumberType can be float, double, long double,..., but not string, unsigned int, int,...</p>

answer:
To convert the string `word` to the desired `NumberType`, you can use the `std::stoi`, `std::stof`, or `std::stold` functions depending on whether you need integer, floating-point, or long floating-point values, respectively. Here's an example of how you can modify your `read` function to handle different types:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
#include <sstream>
#include <vector>

template <typename NumberType = double>
class A
{
public:
    A() {}
    ~A() {}

    void read(std::ifstream& fileIn)
    {
        std::string line, word;
        unsigned int row = 0;
        while (std::getline(fileIn, line))
        {
            std::istringstream lineStream(line);
            unsigned int col = 0;
            while (std::getline(lineStream, word, ','))
            {
                if constexpr (std::is_same_v<NumberType, double>) {
                    matrix_[row][col] = std::stod(word);
                } else if constexpr (std::is_same_v<NumberType, float>) {
                    matrix_[row][col] = std::stof(word);
                } else if constexpr (std::is_same_v<NumberType, long double>) {
                    matrix_[row][col] = std::stold(word);
                } else {
                    throw std::invalid_argument("Unsupported number type");
                }
                col++;
            }
            row++;
        }
    }

private:
    std::vector<std::vector<NumberType>> matrix_;
};

int main()
{
    A<double> doubleMatrix;
    doubleMatrix.read(std::ifstream("data.csv"));

    // You can now use doubleMatrix as needed

    return 0;
}
```

### Explanation:
1. **Template Parameter**: The template parameter `NumberType` allows you to specify the type of numbers stored in the matrix.
2. **Conditional Compilation**: Using `constexpr if` statements, we check the type of `NumberType` at compile time using `std::is_same_v`. This ensures that the correct conversion function (`std::stod`, `std::stof`, or `std::stold`) is used.
3. **Error Handling**: If `NumberType` does not match any of the supported types, a `std::invalid_argument` is thrown.

This approach allows you to easily change the `NumberType` without modifying the rest of the code, making it more flexible and reusable.

