Title: same method or variable name in a javascript class behaves differently.
Body: <pre><code>class Parent{
    method(){
        console.log(&quot;Parent&quot;);
    }
}
class Child extends Parent{
    method(){
        console.log(&quot;Parent&quot;);
    }
}
var child = new Child();
console.log(child.method);
</code></pre>
<p>console return method in child class which is a expected behaviour.</p>
<pre><code>class Parent{
    method = &quot;sss&quot;
}
class Child extends Parent{
    method(){
        console.log(&quot;Child&quot;)
    }
}
var child = new Child();
console.log(child.method)
</code></pre>
<p>why does the console return method variable - &quot;sss&quot; in Parent class ?</p>

answer:
In JavaScript, methods defined inside classes can either be regular functions (default) or arrow functions. When you define a method as an arrow function, it captures the `this` context of the enclosing scope, not the class instance.

Here's how the two scenarios work:

1. **Regular Function Method**:
   ```javascript
   class Parent {
       method() {
           console.log("Parent");
       }
   }

   class Child extends Parent {
       method() {
           console.log("Parent");
       }
   }

   var child = new Child();
   console.log(child.method); // Outputs "Parent"
   ```
   In this case, both the `method` in the `Parent` class and the `method` in the `Child` class point to the same function, so calling `child.method()` logs "Parent".

2. **Arrow Function Method**:
   ```javascript
   class Parent {
       method = "sss";
   }

   class Child extends Parent {
       method() {
           console.log("Child");
       }
   }

   var child = new Child();
   console.log(child.method); // Outputs "sss"
   ```
   Here, the `method` in the `Child` class is defined using an arrow function, which captures the `this` context from the `Parent` class. Since `this` refers to the `Child` instance (`child`), calling `child.method()` logs "Child". The value `"sss"` is stored in the `method` property of the `Parent` class, but it's accessed through the `Child` instance.

This behavior is different because arrow functions do not have their own `this` context, they inherit the `this` context from the enclosing scope.

