Title: Why does the `instanceof` operator return false on instance passed to library? (No inheritance involved).
Body: <p>I am building a library with TS. This library uses the <code>ssh2</code> library as a dependency.</p>
<p>I'm trying to create a function that can either accept an <code>ssh2</code> configuration object or an already existing <code>Client</code> instance to execute a command (this is a simplified case):</p>
<pre><code>import { Client, ConnectConfig } from 'ssh2';

export function runCommand(params: Client | ConnectConfig) {
  if(params instanceof Client) {
    // do something
  } else {
    // create our own client
    // do something
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>When I build this library and call the function like so:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const { readFileSync } = require(&quot;fs&quot;);
const { Client } = require(&quot;ssh2&quot;);
const { runCommand } = require(&quot;myLib&quot;);
const conn = new Client();

conn
  .on(&quot;ready&quot;, () =&gt; {
    console.log(&quot;Client :: ready&quot;);
    runCommand(conn);
  })
  .connect({
    host: &quot;example.com&quot;,
    port: 22,
    username: &quot;me&quot;,
    privateKey: readFileSync(process.env.HOME + &quot;/.ssh/id_ed25519&quot;),
  });
</code></pre>
<p>the <code>instanceof</code> check in my function will return <code>false</code> for some reason. What exactly is happening here? Is it a TS compilation issue or does Node.js see the <code>Client</code> from my lib's dependency and my consumer code's dependency as two different classes?</p>
<p>The compiled code looks something like:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const ssh2_1 = require(&quot;ssh2&quot;);
//...
function runCommand(params) {
    if (params instanceof ssh2_1.Client) {
        // do something
    }
    else {
       // create our own client
    // do something
    }
}
</code></pre>

answer:
The issue you're encountering is due to TypeScript's type checking rules and how it handles imports and dependencies. When you import `ssh2` using `require`, it returns a CommonJS module, which is not treated as a subtype of the TypeScript type `Client`. 

Here's why this happens:

1. **CommonJS Module**: The `ssh2` module imported with `require` is a CommonJS module. In JavaScript, CommonJS modules have a global scope, meaning they don't have a separate namespace for each file. Therefore, the `Client` type from `ssh2` might not be available globally in your TypeScript project.

2. **TypeScript Type Checking**: TypeScript uses its own type system to infer types at compile time. When you use `require('ssh2')`, TypeScript doesn't know what `ssh2` exports, so it treats it as any other module. As a result, the `instanceof` check `params instanceof ssh2_1.Client` fails because `ssh2_1.Client` is not defined in the context where the code is being executed.

To resolve this issue, you need to ensure that the `ssh2` module you are importing is compatible with TypeScript. Here are a few steps you can take:

### Option 1: Use ES6 Modules

If possible, switch to using ES6 modules by changing your import statement to:

```javascript
import { Client, ConnectConfig } from 'ssh2';
```

This will allow TypeScript to recognize the `ssh2` module as a valid type and perform type checking correctly.

### Option 2: Define a Custom Type

Alternatively, you can define a custom type that represents the `ssh2` module and then use that type in your function signature. This approach ensures that TypeScript knows the exact shape of the `ssh2` module and can properly perform type checking.

```typescript
interface ISsh2 {
  Client: typeof Client;
  ConnectConfig: typeof ConnectConfig;
}

import { Client, ConnectConfig } from 'ssh2';
import { runCommand } from './myLib'; // Adjust the path accordingly

const { readFileSync } = require('fs');
const { ssh2 } = require('./ssh2'); // Ensure this path matches the actual location of the ssh2 module

runCommand(ssh2);

const conn = new ssh2();

conn.on('ready', () => {
  console.log('Client :: ready');
  runCommand(conn);
});
```

In this example, we've created an interface `ISsh2` that includes the `Client` and `ConnectConfig` types from the `ssh2` module. We then use this interface in our function signature and import the `ssh2` module using the relative path.

### Option 3: Use a Library Wrapper

If you prefer to stick with CommonJS modules and still want to use TypeScript, you can create a wrapper function that checks if the `ssh2` module is available and provides a fallback mechanism if it isn't.

```typescript
function getSSH2(): ISsh2 | undefined {
  try {
    const { Client, ConnectConfig } = require('ssh2');
    return { Client, ConnectConfig };
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Failed to load ssh2:', error);
    return undefined;
  }
}

export function runCommand(params: Client | ConnectConfig | undefined) {
  if (params && params instanceof Client) {
    // do something
  } else if (params && params instanceof ConnectConfig) {
    // do something
  } else if (params) {
    const ssh2 = getSSH2();
    if (ssh2) {
      // do something with ssh2
    } else {
      // handle the case where ssh2 is not available
    }
  }
}
```

In this example, we define a `getSSH2` function that attempts to import the `ssh2` module and returns it wrapped in an interface. We then use this function in our `runCommand` function to safely access the `Client` and `ConnectConfig` types.

