To understand the difference between `new()` and `new class()`, we need to delve into how these keywords work in C#. The first keyword, `new()`, creates a new instance of a class without passing any arguments. This means that the constructor of the class will be called with no parameters.

Here is an example:

```csharp
public class Human
{
    public string Name { get; set; }

    public Human()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("A human was created.");
    }
}

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        Human john1 = new(); // Calls the default constructor
        Human john2 = new Human(); // Calls the default constructor again
    }
}
```

In this case, both `john1` and `john2` will output "A human was created." because they both call the default constructor of the `Human` class.

Now, let's consider the second way of creating a new instance using `new class()`. This is generally used when you want to create an instance of a generic type or a type parameterized by a specific type. Here is an example:

```csharp
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class GenericList<T>
{
    private List<T> _list;

    public GenericList()
    {
        _list = new List<T>();
    }

    public void Add(T item)
    {
        _list.Add(item);
    }

    public void Remove(T item)
    {
        _list.Remove(item);
    }
}

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        GenericList<int> list1 = new GenericList<int>(); // Creates a new instance of GenericList<int>
        GenericList<double> list2 = new GenericList<double>(); // Creates a new instance of GenericList<double>

        list1.Add(42);
        list2.Add(3.14);

        Console.WriteLine(list1.Count); // Outputs 1
        Console.WriteLine(list2.Count); // Outputs 1
    }
}
```

In this case, `list1` and `list2` will both output "1" because they both call the default constructor of the `GenericList` class, which initializes the internal list.

So, to summarize:
- `new()` creates a new instance of a class without passing any arguments.
- `new class()` creates a new instance of a generic type or a type parameterized by a specific type.

Both methods can be used interchangeably depending on your use case.

