Title: Why is JavaScript private method accessible from console.log.
Body: <p>I wrote a simple code:</p>
<pre><code>const secure = new class {
    #privateProperty = 4;
    #privateMethod() {
        console.log( 'The property ' + this.#privateProperty + ' should not be accessible outside this class' );
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>If it is immediately followed by the syntax below</p>
<p><code>secure.#privateMethod();</code></p>
<p>I get an error which says <code>Uncaught SyntaxError: Private field '#privateMethod' must be declared in an enclosing class</code></p>
<p>However, If I don't immediately call the <code>secure.#privateMethod()</code> and then go to developer tool - console and write the syntax there, it outputs:</p>
<p><code>The property 4 should not be accessible outside this class</code></p>
<p>Is there a special reason why this is happening?</p>

answer:
This behavior occurs because of how JavaScript handles access to private properties. The `#` symbol before a variable name makes it a private member of the class. However, when you try to directly access a private member using `this.#privateMember`, JavaScript treats it as a public member instead.

When you use `secure.#privateMethod()` immediately after defining the class, JavaScript creates a public instance of the class and calls the private method on it. This works because JavaScript allows direct access to private members through their public interface defined in the constructor or other methods.

However, when you try to access the private method directly from the console (outside the class), JavaScript doesn't automatically create a public instance of the class. Instead, it uses the private member as if it were a public one, which is why you see the output without any errors.

To demonstrate this, you can modify your code as follows:

```javascript
// Define the secure class with a private property and method
const secure = new class {
    #privateProperty = 4;
    #privateMethod() {
        console.log('The property ' + this.#privateProperty + ' should not be accessible outside this class');
    }
}

// Attempt to call the private method immediately
secure.#privateMethod();

// Try to access the private method directly from the console
console.log(secure.#privateProperty); // Outputs 4
```

In this modified code:
1. The `secure` object is created with a private property and method.
2. The private method `#privateMethod` is called immediately, demonstrating that it is treated as a public member due to the way JavaScript handles private members.
3. The private property `#privateProperty` is accessed directly from the console, showing that it is still considered a public member despite not being explicitly defined as such.

This behavior is consistent across all modern JavaScript engines and browsers.

