Title: std::sort invocation causes subscript out of range compile-time error.
Body: <p>The below code fails to compile with error: &quot;array subscript 16 is outside array bounds of …&quot;.
I don't understand why, <code>w</code> is guaranteed to be &lt;= arr1.size().</p>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/5n5KohsnW" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://godbolt.org/z/5n5KohsnW</a></p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;array&gt;
#include &lt;algorithm&gt;
#include &lt;tuple&gt;

extern bool test_func();

int tempfunc() 
{
    std::array&lt;std::tuple&lt;double,int&gt;,5&gt; arr1;
    int w = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 5; ++i ) {
        if( test_func()) {
            arr1[w] = {3.,4};
            w++;
        }
    }
    std::sort(arr1.begin(), arr1.begin() + w);

    return 0;
}
</code></pre>

answer:
The provided C++ code snippet encounters a runtime error due to an attempt to access an element in the `std::array` at index `w`, which is greater than the size of the array. This results in a subscript out of range exception.

To resolve this issue, you need to ensure that the value of `w` does not exceed the size of the array before attempting to access it. Here's how you can modify the code:

```cpp
#include <array>
#include <algorithm>
#include <tuple>

extern bool test_func();

int tempfunc() 
{
    std::array<std::tuple<double,int>,5> arr1;
    int w = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < 5; ++i ) {
        if( test_func()) {
            arr1[w] = {3.,4};
            ++w;
        }
    }

    // Ensure w does not exceed the size of arr1
    if (w <= arr1.size()) {
        std::sort(arr1.begin(), arr1.begin() + w);
    } else {
        std::cerr << "Error: w exceeds the size of arr1." << std::endl;
        return -1;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In this modified version, we added an `if` statement that checks if `w` is less than or equal to the size of `arr1`. If `w` is within the valid range, we proceed with sorting. Otherwise, we print an error message and return a non-zero value to indicate an error condition.

By ensuring that `w` is always within the bounds of the array, you can avoid the subscript out of range error and successfully sort the elements of the array.

