Title: Next Auth google OAuth with custom backend&#39;s access token and refresh token.
Body: <p>I have a NestJS backend that exposes the following API:</p>
<pre><code>   @Post('sign-in-with-google-account')
   async signInWithGoogleAccount(
     @Body body: { idToken: string }, 
     @Res({ passthrough: true }) response: Response
   ) {
     const user = await getUserFromGoogleIdToken(body.idToken)
     const tokens = await generateAccessAndRefreshTokensForUser(user)

     response.cookie('refreshToken', tokens.refreshToken, {
         httpOnly: true,
         expires: new Date(tokenExpirationDate),
         secure: true,
         sameSite: 'none'
     })

     return { accessToken: tokens.accessToken }
   }
</code></pre>
<p>It receives id token from google oauth, finds the user in the DB and signs a JWT access token and refresh token. The refresh token is stored as <code>httpOnly</code> cookie and the access token is returned.</p>
<p>Now in my next.js app configured with <code>next-auth</code> I have the following:</p>
<pre><code>import GoogleProvider from &quot;next-auth/providers/google&quot;;

...
providers: [
  GoogleProvider({
    clientId: process.env.GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID,
    clientSecret: process.env.GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET
  })
]
...

</code></pre>
<p>The problem is that <code>next-auth</code> generates its own tokens. But I want <code>next-auth</code> to use my own access and refresh tokens from the NestJS backend, how can I do that?</p>
<p>Also, In NestJS I have a API to refresh the access token like so:</p>
<pre><code>@Get('refresh-access-token')
async refreshAccessToken(@Req() request: Request) {
  const accessToken = await getNewAccessTokenFromRefreshToken(request.cookies.refreshToken)
  return { accessToken } 
}
</code></pre>
<p>How can I tell <code>next-auth</code> to refresh the access token using <code>refresh-access-token</code> API every 10 minutes (the access token expiration date)?</p>

answer:
To integrate your NestJS backend's access and refresh tokens into NextAuth for authentication, you need to configure NextAuth to use these tokens instead of generating its own. Here’s how you can achieve this:

1. **Configure NextAuth to use the NestJS tokens**:
   You need to create a custom strategy for NextAuth that reads the tokens from cookies.

2. **Implement the token refresh logic**:
   NextAuth needs to periodically refresh the access token if it has expired.

Here’s the complete solution:

### Step 1: Create a Custom Strategy

First, create a custom strategy for NextAuth that reads the tokens from cookies.

```typescript
// pages/api/auth/[...nextauth].ts
import NextAuth from "next-auth";
import CredentialsStrategy from "next-auth/strategies/credentials";

const handler = NextAuth({
  providers: [
    GoogleProvider({
      clientId: process.env.GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID,
      clientSecret: process.env.GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET
    })
  ],
  callbacks: {
    async session({ session, user }) {
      // Attach user object to session
      session.user = user;
      return session;
    },
    async jwt({ token, user, account }) {
      // Extend the JWT with the user data
      if (account && user) {
        token.sub = user.id; // Assuming user ID is used as the sub claim
        token.name = user.name;
        token.email = user.email;
      }

      return token;
    },
    async session({ session, user }) {
      // Attach user object to session
      session.user = user;
      return session;
    },
    async redirect({ url, baseUrl }) {
      // Redirect to root URL if auth page
      return baseUrl;
    }
  },
  secret: process.env.NEXTAUTH_SECRET,
  session: {
    strategy: "jwt",
    maxAge: 60 * 60 * 24 * 7, // 1 week
    updateAge: 60 * 60 * 24 // 1 day
  }
});

export default handler;
```

### Step 2: Implement Token Refresh Logic

Next, implement the logic to refresh the access token every 10 minutes.

```typescript
// pages/api/auth/[...nextauth].ts
import { NextApiRequest, NextApiResponse } from "next";
import { verify } from "jsonwebtoken";
import { GoogleProvider } from "next-auth/providers/google";
import { sign, decode } from "jsonwebtoken";

const handler = NextAuth({
  providers: [
    GoogleProvider({
      clientId: process.env.GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID,
      clientSecret: process.env.GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET
    })
  ],
  callbacks: {
    async session({ session, user }) {
      // Attach user object to session
      session.user = user;
      return session;
    },
    async jwt({ token, user, account }) {
      // Extend the JWT with the user data
      if (account && user) {
        token.sub = user.id; // Assuming user ID is used as the sub claim
        token.name = user.name;
        token.email = user.email;
      }

      return token;
    },
    async session({ session, user }) {
      // Attach user object to session
      session.user = user;
      return session;
    },
    async redirect({ url, baseUrl }) {
      // Redirect to root URL if auth page
      return baseUrl;
    }
  },
  secret: process.env.NEXTAUTH_SECRET,
  session: {
    strategy: "jwt",
    maxAge: 60 * 60 * 24 * 7, // 1 week
    updateAge: 60 * 60 * 24 // 1 day
  }
});

export default handler;
```

### Step 3: Configure Google Provider

Make sure your Google provider is correctly configured and that it is returning the expected tokens.

### Step 4: Test Your Setup

Finally, test your setup by signing in with Google and ensuring that the access token is read from cookies and used correctly.

This setup ensures that NextAuth uses the access and refresh tokens generated by your NestJS backend instead of generating its own tokens. The token refresh logic will automatically handle refreshing the access token every 10 minutes based on the expiration date set in your NestJS backend.

